.TH EUCLID-CLI-EMO 1 "October 2026" "euclid-cli 2.1.3" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-emo \- Euclid monitoring: the metrics it records, and threshold alerts on them
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli
[\fB\-\-endpoint\fR \fIURL\fR]
[\fB\-\-loglevel\fR \fILEVEL\fR]
.B emo
.I action
[\fIaction-options\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B emo
module is where Euclid keeps its metrics: what every module, every
application and every host reports about itself, averaged into one row
per series every period \- five minutes as shipped
.RI ( euclid.modules.emo.average-period ).
Rows are rolled up into hourly and daily tiers and kept for 7 days, 90
days and 5 years respectively.
.PP
From the command line it does two things: it says which metrics exist,
and it raises alerts when one of them crosses a line.
.SH METRICS AND SERIES
A metric is a name \-
.IR system-cpu-usage ,
.IR module-backlog ,
.I disk-available
\- and a series is one metric with one set of labels: the CPU of host
.IR vogje01-pios ,
the backlog of module
.IR eqs .
.BR euclid-cli-emo-list-metrics (1)
lists the names, how many series each has and which labels tell them
apart. Those names and labels are what an alert rule is written in.
.PP
Each row carries three figures: a
.I value
\- the period's mean for a gauge, its sum for a rate \- and the period's
.I min
and
.IR max .
.SH ALERTING
An alert rule names a metric, optionally narrows it to the series whose
labels match, and sets a warning line, a critical line, or both. Every
period, as the rows are written, each series the rule matches is judged
and stays in, or moves between, four states:
.TP
.B OK
Inside the lines.
.TP
.B WARNING
Past the warning line, not the critical one.
.TP
.B CRITICAL
Past the critical line.
.TP
.B NO_DATA
The series stopped arriving \- only for a rule that asks for it.
.PP
A state is entered only after the value has been past its line for
.I forPeriods
periods running, and left only after it has been back inside for
.I clearPeriods
\- so one spike is not an alert, and a value sitting on a line does not
raise and clear one every five minutes. A CRITICAL series whose value
settles between the lines drops back to WARNING.
.PP
Each change of state \- and only a change; a hundred periods of
CRITICAL are one \- is recorded, and published on the event bus as
.I emo.alert.raised
or
.IR emo.alert.cleared .
Anything can act on it by subscribing through
.BR euclid-cli-ees (1);
.I name
and
.I severity
are top-level fields of the event, so a subscription can take only one
metric's, or only the critical ones. Who is woken, and how, is the
subscriber's business, not EMO's.
.PP
Rules are evaluated on the rows EMO writes, never on the samples it is
pushed, and only on the five-minute tier: an hourly or daily rule would
be a report, not an alert.
.SH ACTIONS
.TP
.B list-metrics
Every metric there is, by name. See
.BR euclid-cli-emo-list-metrics (1).
.TP
.B create-alert-rule
A threshold rule on one metric. See
.BR euclid-cli-emo-create-alert-rule (1).
.TP
.B update-alert-rule
Change a rule. See
.BR euclid-cli-emo-update-alert-rule (1).
.TP
.B delete-alert-rule
Delete a rule, clearing what it raised. See
.BR euclid-cli-emo-delete-alert-rule (1).
.TP
.B list-alert-rules
The rules. See
.BR euclid-cli-emo-list-alert-rules (1).
.TP
.B list-alerts
Where every judged series stands now. See
.BR euclid-cli-emo-list-alerts (1).
.TP
.B list-alert-history
Every change of state, newest first. See
.BR euclid-cli-emo-list-alert-history (1).
.SH AUTHENTICATION
All
.B emo
commands require the caller to be authenticated; run
.B euclid-cli eam login
first. They also require an administrator: the metrics describe the
whole installation, not one account's part of it, and an alert's state
says what they are. Rule changes are audited \- see
.BR euclid-cli-ead (1).
.SH CONFIGURATION
.TP
.I euclid.modules.emo.average-period
Seconds per row, and so per alert evaluation. 300 as shipped.
.TP
.I euclid.modules.emo.alert.max-series-per-rule
How many series one rule may judge. A rule matching more is refused when
it is created. 200 as shipped.
.TP
.I euclid.modules.emo.alert.history-retention
Days a recorded change of state is kept. 90 as shipped.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
# Which metrics exist
euclid-cli emo list-metrics --names-only

# Warn at 80% CPU on any host, critical at 90%
euclid-cli emo create-alert-rule --rule-id system-cpu-per-host \\
    --metric system-cpu-usage --warning 80 --critical 90 --clear-periods 2

# What is wrong right now
euclid-cli emo list-alerts --active
.fi
.RE
